Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
speaker.md
## Magnetic-point group
Magnetic point group gives at a 6-fold screw axis (rotation combined with translation and time inversion). 

The simplest case if we induce an external magnetic field B || z, it reduces the symmetry to 3m. 

This gives us the following shapes of the dielectric tensor, magnetic and magneto-electric responses. For B || z we get non-diagonal tensor elements - antisymmetric with respect to time reversal.

To fill them up, we need a response function. For that we use linear response - or the Kubo formula.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Fade: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide
<!-- .slide: class="slide-standard slide-dark" data-background-color="black" -->

<MakeTitle>Neumann and Curie Principle</MakeTitle>

- AFM state ⟶ 6<sup>'</sup><sub>3</sub>m<sup>'</sup>m
- <span class="fragment">AFM state and B||z ⟶ 3m<sup>'</sup></span>

  
<div class="fragment mt-2">Material equations:</div>

<div class="fragment">
$$
\\mathbf{P} = \\epsilon_0\\Big(\\hat{\\varepsilon} \\mathbf{E} + \\hat{\\chi}^{(me)} \\mathbf{H}\\Big) \\\\
\\mathbf{M} = \\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\Big(\\hat{\\mu} \\mathbf{H} - \\hat{\\chi}^{(me)} \\mathbf{E} \\Big)
$$
</div>


$$
	\hat{\varepsilon}=\left( \begin{array}{ccc}	
	\varepsilon_{xx}^{(S)} & \varepsilon_{xy}^{(A)} & 0\\\\
	-\varepsilon_{xy}^{(A)} & \varepsilon_{xx}^{(S)} & 0\\\\
	0 & 0 & \varepsilon_{zz}^{(S)}\\\\
	\end{array}\right), \\quad \hat{\mu}=\left( \begin{array}{ccc}	
	\mu_{xx}^{(S)} & \mu_{xy}^{(A)} & 0\\\\
	-\mu_{xy}^{(A)} & \mu_{xx}^{(S)} & 0\\\\
	0 & 0 & \mu_{zz}^{(S)}\\\\
	\end{array}\right), \\\\
\text{}
 \\\\
 \hat{\chi}^{(me)}=\left( \begin{array}{ccc}	
	\chi_{xx}^{(S)} & \chi_{xy}^{(A)} & 0\\\\
	-\chi_{xy}^{(A)} & \chi_{xx}^{(S)} & 0\\\\
	0 & 0 & \chi_{zz}^{(S)}\\\\
	\end{array}\right)
$$ <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->

<Footer/>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
FigModel = NotebookRead[NotebookStore["opus-a5f"]];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
speaker.md
## Linear response
We can say, ok. Every mode in ground state is doubly degenerate, when we apply the field we should see Zeeman's effect, that resolves it into two branches. Then it must be just two modes, which share the same parameters. Expect the eigen energy is offset by some number - delta.

And for each of the oscillators we can estimate the magnitude using two matrix elements: electric p and magnetic m dipole transition amplitudes.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide
<!-- .slide: class="slide-standard slide-dark" data-background-color="black" -->

<MakeTitle>Zeeman Effect</MakeTitle>

<Columns>

<FigModel/>

<div>

- 2 oscillators per mode
- 3 contributions:
  - electric $p^2 \\rightarrow \\varepsilon$
  - magnetic $m^2 \\rightarrow \\mu$
  - magneto-electric $p m \\rightarrow \\chi$

<br/>
<br/>
<br/>

- use Kubo formula to get $\\varepsilon$, $\\mu$ and $\\chi$
</div>

</Columns>

<Footer/>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
speaker.md
## Solution of Maxwell's equations
Then Maxwell's equations gives 4 possible waves with 4 refractive indexes:

- CW, CCW, and +/- k - propagation direction

It might look horribly for you, but you can find that this only depends on 4 real-valued numbers. Basically we reduced 27 complex tensor components to 4 numbers, that you can measure in zero field and then you know everything about your system.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide
<!-- .slide: class="slide-standard slide-dark" data-background-color="black" -->

<MakeTitle>Solution of Maxwell's Equations</MakeTitle>
For the light propagating along the field direction $k|| B ||z$:

$$
\\begin{align*}
n_{\circlearrowleft}^{\pm k}&=\sqrt{\left(1+\frac{4 m^2 \omega _0}{\omega _0^2-(\delta +i \eta +\omega )^2}\right) \left(\varepsilon _0^{(xx)}+\frac{4 p^2 \omega _0}{\omega _0^2-(\delta +i \eta +\omega )^2}\right)}\pm\frac{4 \delta  m p}{-\omega _0^2+(\delta +i \eta +\omega )^2} \\\\
n\_{\circlearrowright}^{\pm k}&=\sqrt{\left(1+\frac{4 m^2 \omega _0}{\omega _0^2-(-\delta +i \eta +\omega )^2}\right) \left(\varepsilon _0^{(xx)}+\frac{4 p^2 \omega _0}{\omega _0^2-(-\delta +i \eta +\omega )^2}\right)}\mp\frac{4 \delta  m p}{-\omega _0^2+(-\delta +i \eta +\omega )^2}
\\end{align*}
$$

Here: 

- $\delta = g~|B_z|$;
- $m,p$ are real-valued numbers - measured at __0 field__;
- 4 real-valued parameters define all optical properties!

- for any transmitted light: <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->

$$
\\begin{align*}
S_{\circlearrowright/\circlearrowleft}^{(\\pm k)}(\omega) \propto exp\Big(-\frac{i n_{\circlearrowright/\circlearrowleft}^{(\\pm k)} \omega L}{c}\Big)
\\end{align*}
$$ <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->

- eigenstates of light are circular ⟳⟳ <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->


