speaker.md
## How to measure circular polarized light?
It is not obvious how to do that. We need two measurements with the analyzer set to +/- 45 degrees from the polarizer, then we recombine the signal to get circular polarized basis. And then take the ratio.

To disentangle Faraday effect or MCD from other non-reciprocal effects, we enforce it has to be odd in B and even in k.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Actual slide:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Actual slide:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide
<!-- .slide: class="slide-standard slide-dark" data-background-color="black" -->

<MakeTitle>How to measure ⟳ polarized light?</MakeTitle>

<Columns>

<img class="dark-content" src="/attachments/Screenshot-2026-03-10-at-16.49.00-64a.png" width="400"/>

<div>


- $S_{\circlearrowright/\circlearrowleft}(\omega) = S_{+45} \\pm i(S_{-45})$
- Take the ratio: $S = S_{\circlearrowright}/S_{\circlearrowleft}$

<br/><br/>

Signal extraction (odd in B, even in k): <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->

$$
S_{MCD/F} = \left\\{\frac{S^{(+k,+B)} \cdot S^{(-k,+B)}}{S^{(+k,-B)} \cdot S^{(-k,-B)}} \right\\}^{1/4}
$$ <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->
  
</div>

</Columns>


