speaker.md
### Faraday
Now if we look at phase - take the argument of it, we get Faraday rotation angle. Here it is at fixed magnetic field.

And look, if we stay between two modes, we can still have linear polarized light but the rotation angle is 6000 degrees per cm. This is large, much larger than a conventional antiferromagnet.

Now if we use our model derived from magnetic-point group analysis:

you see it perfectly lines up with experimental data. Even though we used zero-field data for oscillator parameters. 

For the physics: but it gives large angle: magnetic and electric dipole transitions boosted by polarizability of the crystal - epsilon_infty.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Faraday and other effects:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide
<!-- .slide: class="slide-standard slide-dark" data-background-color="black" -->

<MakeTitle><strong>Our Data</strong>: Faraday Effect</MakeTitle>

<Columns>

<div class="dark-content">
  <FigFaradayWidget/>
</div>

<div style="margin-top:5rem">

$$
\theta_F(\omega) = \frac{\omega L}{c}arg(S_{MCD/F}(\omega))
$$

<br/><br/>

- large Faraday rotation: $\color{red}6000~{}^\circ/\text{cm}$
- for conventional antiferromagnets like EuTiO<sub>3</sub>$\\sim 2500\~{}^\circ/\text{cm}$ (GHz), FePS<sub>3</sub>$\\sim 600\~{}^\circ/\text{cm}$ (THz)
  
</div>

</Columns>

From our model: <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->

$$
\theta_{F} = \\Re ~ \frac{\omega L}{2c} \Bigg(\sqrt{\left(1+\frac{4 m^2 \omega _0}{\omega _0^2-(\delta +i \eta -\omega )^2}\right) \left(\varepsilon\_\infty^{(xx)}+\frac{4 p^2 \omega _0}{\omega _0^2-(\delta +i \eta -\omega )^2}\right)} 
$$ <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->

From zero-field data: <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->

$$ 
p = 0.31, ~m = 0.22, ~\varepsilon_\infty^{(xx)} = 22.4
$$<!-- .element: class="fragment" -->

- polar structure gives a boost to magnetic-dipole transitions <!-- .element: class="fragment" -->



